 # Scrap League implementation and release status
 
+## Handbook follow-up — October 17, 2026
+
+This pass addresses concrete gaps against handbook 2.8.0. Driver names are editable from Leaderboards and Settings and persist on the device. Result submission opens independently of board availability, preserves invalid input, shows durable queue/confirmed/rejected state and retains the original submitted name. Explicit retry respects server delays and tab leases; a slow expired lease cannot be reclaimed repeatedly by one pump. Optional cache-write failure no longer hides a successful board read. Refresh preserves displayed matching times until replacement.
+
+The 320px, 200%-computed-font check found and corrected horizontal overflow in the leaderboard dialog. Touch Boost includes throttle, so steering and boosting fit two contacts in manual mode. Resize and keyboard focus changes cancel affected held input. The PWA explicitly checks for updates on foreground/reconnect, limits repeated checks, offers a visible update-ready button, and retains explicit safe-boundary activation. In-game update notices do not imply operating-system notifications while closed.
+
+`audit/2026-10-17-handbook-followup.md` maps the guide to implementation and evidence. `audit/handbook-unit-tests.txt` records 107 passing tests. Current browser fixtures cover the complete twelve-event campaign with a between-round reload and held muted/reduced-motion ending, a newly authored closed circuit, quota failure followed by navigation/export/retry, five-AI test/return, improved solo best/ghost, name entry/queuing, controller interruption and responsive reflow. Exact receipts and failed fixture attempts are retained individually. The live public-board probe at 21:11 UTC passed all Trial/Daily checks and conditionally removed its four accepted synthetic rows; earlier intermittent provider failures remain historical evidence, not a resolved root-cause claim.
+
+The current acceptance ledger remains authoritative for scope. Actual browser-process restart and a provider-wide policy readiness API are unavailable; original circuit authorship cannot be changed retroactively. The follow-up does not silently waive these or other incomplete broad release rows. Publication and exact live readback are recorded separately in `audit/deployment.json`.
+
+Performance checks found and corrected excess Canvas strokes: static curb geometry now compiles once per track and skid marks draw in one batch. Default resolution and simulation are unchanged. The optimized release completed a six-car race at p95/p99 16.8/16.9 ms with a 1920×753 buffer and full effects. The earlier twenty-minute lower-resolution run covered 71,846 frames and 40 loads at 17.1/17.4 ms; only the renderer changed afterward. Those scopes are distinct, and both failed default-resolution attempts remain retained. Available JS heap samples in the long run ranged from 14.5 to 29.2 MB; they do not measure total working set or GPU memory.
+
+Current live endpoint fixtures confirm catalog-conflict/direct-link recovery, safe listing retry, lost owner-response readback, and two independent queue clients reconciling one accepted public score after its response is dropped. Populated Workshop/catalog and board fixtures are kept across deployment and conditionally cleaned afterward; old-client dependency readback is recorded separately. A public seed still returned an intermittent 500 before succeeding, so provider reliability is not presented as solved by client changes.
+
 ## Arcade remake — October 17, 2026
 
 The current source replaces the WebGL racing view with an overhead Canvas 2D game. The home screen starts a two-lap six-car Quick Race in one action. Touch controls have independent pointer ownership and reserved space outside the play area, including manual GAS, brake, slide and boost. Road-ahead prompts, a visible YOU marker and action-based Driving school improve first-use guidance. League, all twelve circuits, 81 builds, ghosts, Daily, existing saves, public times and the bundled Workshop remain available.
